// Ambiguous-failure retry, licensed by a property — the acceptance test of
// docs/design/rfc-properties.md phase 5 (epistemics.md: "retry an effect ⇒
// know it did not apply, or make the retry itself produce knowledge").
//
// Every failure of an effectful boundary call is one of two kinds
// (NetworkError.definite): DEFINITE — the effect is known not to have
// applied, react freely — or AMBIGUOUS — the request left the process and
// no response returned; the effect may or may not have applied. Retrying
// from ambiguity is only sound when re-applying is harmless: idempotency.
//
// The language hardcodes none of this. `idempotent` is a property declared
// in std.verify; the retry combinator REQUIRES it of its function-typed
// argument (`fn(string) int! provides verify.idempotent` — the same type
// surface that carries the fallibility contract `!`); the provider CLAIMS
// it. Today the only legitimate claim is at a trust boundary: std.verify's
// `idempotent` is declared-only (its proof shape — a dedup check dominating
// the effect — is not yet expressible in the kernel, rfc-properties.md
// phase 5.5), so an in-unit `provides idempotent(...)` is a compile error
// pointing here, and the extern `assume` below is the one honest discharge.
// `pluto analyze` lists every such assumption: the program's complete
// assumption surface, with a named owner per claim.
import std.verify

// ── The trust boundary ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Stand-in for an external service's idempotent PUT (bound to a runtime
// builtin so the example runs self-contained; think "S3 put, keyed on the
// request"). The compiler cannot see the implementation, so the claim's
// warrant is ASSUMED — explicit, attributed to this declaration, and
// reported by `pluto analyze`, never silently promoted to proven.
extern fn __pluto_string_len(s: string) int assume verify.idempotent(key = s)

error RetryExhausted {
    message: string
}

// ── The combinator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Only functions whose declarations provide `verify.idempotent` flow in:
// passing a plain `fn(string) int` is a compile-time boundary rejection
// ("expected fn(string) int! provides verify.idempotent, found ..."), and
// closures provide nothing. The property is what LICENSES the blind retry
// in the ambiguous branch — delete the requirement and the retry below is
// exactly the classic double-apply bug.
fn with_retry(f: fn(string) int! provides verify.idempotent, req: string) int {
    return f(req) catch e: NetworkError {
        if e.definite {
            // Definite failure: the effect is KNOWN not to have applied.
            // The world is in a known state — report it upward unchanged.
            raise NetworkError { message: e.message, definite: true }
        }
        // Ambiguous failure: the effect may already have applied. No local
        // information can say — but f is idempotent keyed on this request,
        // so re-applying is harmless and the retry's outcome IS knowledge.
        f(req) catch e2 {
            raise RetryExhausted { message: "retry after ambiguous failure also failed" }
            0 // unreachable (raise above)
        }
    } catch other {
        raise RetryExhausted { message: "non-network failure" }
        0 // unreachable (raise above)
    }
}

fn main() {
    let n = with_retry(__pluto_string_len, "pluto") catch e {
        print("request failed definitively")
        0 - 1
    }
    print(f"stored (receipt {n})")
}
